Why should backend servers NOT have public IPs when we already have an internet-facing Load Balancer ?


👉🏻 My answer was simple, and it changed the whole room.


👉🏻 The core idea → 

If the Load Balancer is public, your backend does NOT need to be public → The LB is the gate → It is the only thing that should talk to the outside world. 


👉🏻  Why this matters → 
	→ 	Keeps your servers safe
	→ 	Stops direct attacks
	→ 	Blocks random traffic
	→ 	Cuts down risk
	→ 	Makes the setup clean and easy to control


👉🏻 Real Scenario → 

You host an app → Your ALB/NLB is public → Users hit the LB → LB sends traffic to backend servers in a private subnet.

Now, no user can hit your backend directly → No public IP → No exposure → Only clean, safe flow.
 

👉🏻 Example → 

❌ Bad:
Backend EC2 has a public IP → Anyone can ping → Security risk

✔️ Good:
Public ALB → Sends traffic → Private EC2 with only a private IP → Safe


👉🏻 Final thought → 

→ Your public LB is your shield → Your backend is your engine → Shields face the world → Engines stay hidden.
